{
  "title": "Numbers & Text (Integers and Strings)",
  "subtitle": "Two Core Data Types in Python",
  "topic": "2.2 • DATA TYPES",
  "body": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px; width: 100%;\">\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 4px; width: 100%;\">\n    <div style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px #111111; padding: 4px 14px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 900; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;\">\n      2.2 • Integers vs Strings\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 30px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Numbers vs <hlt>Text</hlt>\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #222222; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 620px;\">\n      Python treats numbers and text very differently. The presence of quotes is what tells Python how to handle the data.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"lesson-ref-card\">\n    <div class=\"lesson-ref-header\">\n      <span class=\"ref-badge\">PREVIOUS KNOWLEDGE</span>\n      <span class=\"lesson-ref-title\">Connecting to Earlier Concepts</span>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"lesson-ref-desc\">\n      In Chapter 2, we learned that text strings are wrapped in double quotes when printed. Let's see how this affects variables!\n    </p>\n    <a href=\"1.html?spcl=1&lsn=2&sub=3&ref_from=1_3_4\" class=\"ref-jump-btn\">Review: Text Strings in print() (Lesson 2.2) →</a>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: #86efac; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 5px 5px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px;\">\n      <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n        Integers (Numbers)\n      </span>\n      <h3 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">NO QUOTES</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.4;\">\n        Used for counts, scores, and math. Stored as raw numeric digits.\n      </p>\n      <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; padding: 10px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 13px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        score = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">100</span><br/>\n        <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(5 + 5)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Outputs: 10</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: #fbcfe8; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 5px 5px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px;\">\n      <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n        Strings (Text)\n      </span>\n      <h3 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">WRAPPED IN QUOTES</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.4;\">\n        Used for names, words, and text. Treated as literal character sequences.\n      </p>\n      <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; padding: 10px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 13px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        player = <span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"John\"</span><br/>\n        <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(<span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"5\"</span> + <span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"5\"</span>)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Outputs: 55</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fde047; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Analogy • ID Card vs Price Tag\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 19px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      You Add Price Tags, Not ID Cards\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      A person's name on an ID card is text—you would never add two names together. A price tag is a number—you add prices together. Quotes are what inform Python: <em>\"Treat this like a name, not a number.\"</em>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n</div>"
}